  "case": "25-patch-content",
  "description": "The `patch` rule (§2), and with it the whole worked scenario authored as DATA with no code at all: a player mantle of body parts and clothing is one rune in the world, an amulet in the world is wired to the player's interface port 1 — which is the shirt's principal, its equip socket — and reducing turns that shirt purple. Before `patch`, the structural half of this was data and the content half was a code-registered `expand` rule, which put the half a designer changes on a Tuesday afternoon behind a recompile (Void Unity's measurement, 2026-08-29). What `patch` pins here: the survivor is chosen by `keep`; `copy` reads a field off the CONSUMED agent (`colour` from the amulet) and `set` writes a literal (`dyed`), with `set` applied last so literals win; the survivor keeps its glyph, arity, tags (`worn`) and aux wiring, so the shirt is still worn on the same skin; its principal is left free, so the socket reopens; and the consumed amulet's aux partner is freed. `pin_ids` carries the other half of the claim — the survivor keeps its own id, `p1/shirt`, because `patch` creates no agent and hands one back. That is a deliberate exception to the derived-id rule and it is what preserves a boxed agent's provenance for free, which is exactly the case a host most often has to draw.",
